<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anton Denikin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Anton Ivanovich Denikin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anton Denikin was a Russian general and a leading commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-movement">White movement</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>. As head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armed-forces-of-south-russia">Armed Forces of South Russia</a> he advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1919, taking much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, but was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> near Oryol and driven back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. He resigned his command in 1920 and died in exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anton-denikin.jpg" alt="Anton Denikin" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denikin1_(cropped).PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It fought the Civil War of 1918–1921 against the White armies of Alexander Kolchak in Siberia, <b>Anton Denikin</b> in the south and Nikolai Yudenich near Petrograd, against the Czechoslovak Legion and against Allied interventions, and defeated Pyotr Wrangel’s last White army in Crimea in November 1920.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In June 1919 <b>Denikin</b>'s Armed Forces of South Russia landed and advanced into the Crimea, and the Soviet government withdrew.</blockquote><a href="/state/crimean-socialist-soviet-republic">Crimean Socialist Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>After Germany's defeat Krasnov resigned in February 1919, and under Ataman Afrikan Bogaevsky the Don joined <b>Denikin</b>'s Armed Forces of South Russia.</blockquote><a href="/state/don-republic">Don Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The insurgents fought Germans, nationalists and Bolsheviks, and in September 1919 their victory at Peregonovka disrupted <b>Denikin</b>'s march on Moscow.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-territory">Free Territory</a></li><li><blockquote>The insurgents fought <b>Denikin</b>'s Whites and then the Red Army, raiding railways and Soviet detachments, and kept contact with the exiled government of the Ukrainian People's Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/kholodny-yar-republic">Kholodny Yar Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>This brought conflict with General <b>Denikin</b>, who wanted a united Russia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kuban-peoples-republic">Kuban People's Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Szpetal. Died in Ann Arbor, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.28,-83.75&amp;year=1920" aria-label="Ann Arbor on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2518 660 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Anton Denikin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2968 930h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2968 930h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=42.28,-83.75&amp;year=1920">What was here in 1920? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
